<section class="hijri-knowledge" id="hijri-guide" aria-labelledby="hijri-guide-heading">
 <header>
  <h2 id="hijri-guide-heading">Hijri date conversion: a complete practical guide</h2>
  <p>Learn what the Islamic lunar calendar measures, compare Umm al-Qura with tabular Hijri calculations, understand the sunset boundary, and plan around Ramadan and Eid without mistaking a software result for a crescent-sighting decision.</p>
 </header>
 <nav class="hijri-guide-nav" aria-label="Hijri conversion guide sections">
  <a href="#hijri-how-to">How to convert</a><a href="#hijri-calendars">Calendar methods</a><a href="#hijri-sunset">Sunset</a><a href="#hijri-months">12 months</a><a href="#hijri-example">Worked example</a><a href="#hijri-questions">FAQs</a><a href="#hijri-sources">Sources</a>
 </nav>
 <div class="hijri-guide-facts">
  <div><span>Hijri year</span><strong>12 lunar months</strong><p>Usually 354 or 355 days in a calculated lunar year</p></div>
  <div><span>Lunar month</span><strong>29 or 30 days</strong><p>Its start varies by calendar convention and observation</p></div>
  <div><span>Religious day</span><strong>Sunset boundary</strong><p>Not automatically the same as the civil midnight change</p></div>
 </div>

 <section class="hijri-guide-section" id="hijri-how-to" aria-labelledby="hijri-how-title">
  <h3 id="hijri-how-title">How to use the Hijri Converter, step by step</h3>
  <ol class="hijri-steps">{steps.map(step=><li><h4>{step.title}</h4><p>{step.description}</p></li>)}</ol>
  <p>The tool works without an account or personal date storage. It can help you prepare a journal entry, check a document or compare two published calendars. When a particular date affects worship, use it as a starting estimate and confirm the accepted local calendar.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="hijri-guide-section" id="hijri-calendars" aria-labelledby="hijri-calendars-title">
  <h3 id="hijri-calendars-title">Umm al-Qura versus civil (tabular) Hijri dates</h3>
  <p>There is not one universal computational definition of an Islamic month. Two calendars may agree for weeks and then differ around a month boundary even though both display an Islamic year, month and day.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="hijri-guide-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">How it assigns months</th><th scope="col">What it cannot establish</th></tr></thead><tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Umm al-Qura</th><td>A calculated month sequence used by the Umm al-Qura calendar, supported in modern date-formatting software.</td><td>Whether a crescent was actually sighted or accepted by a particular local authority.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Civil / tabular Hijri</th><td>A mathematical cycle with a defined leap-year rule and alternating month lengths adjusted within the cycle.</td><td>A country’s observed new-moon date or the religious decision for Ramadan or Eid.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>The converter deliberately names its two methods. The <a href="https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr35/tr35.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Unicode calendar identifiers</a> distinguish <code>islamic-umalqura</code> from <code>islamic-civil</code>. The <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Intl/supportedValuesOf#supported_calendar_types" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">browser calendar documentation</a> describes Umm al-Qura's supported 1300–1600 AH dataset and its fallback outside this range. We limit comparison to that AH span to avoid silently presenting fallback dates as Umm al-Qura dates.</p>
  <p>Both outputs use the date-formatting calendar data on your device. If a device lacks an expected calendar implementation or uses different underlying data, its result may not match a printed calendar. A tabular date is particularly useful as a transparent arithmetic comparison, not as a sighting verdict.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="hijri-guide-section" id="hijri-sunset" aria-labelledby="hijri-sunset-title">
  <h3 id="hijri-sunset-title">Does the Hijri date change at midnight or sunset?</h3>
  <p>A Gregorian civil day normally turns over at local midnight. In Islamic reckoning, the religious date begins with the evening at sunset. That matters if you check the date late in the day, but <strong>a date-only converter does not know whether the Sun has set at your precise location.</strong></p>
  <p>For example, someone looking at a Gregorian Tuesday before Maghrib may refer to one numbered Hijri day; after Maghrib, the following Hijri day has begun in their religious day-counting convention, even though the Gregorian date is still Tuesday. Use the optional “next lunar day” switch only after establishing the local sunset boundary yourself.</p>
  <p>Advancing a day in software <strong>does not simulate crescent visibility</strong>. Separately, local or national authorities may announce that the next lunar month starts on a different evening. The calendar used for worship should follow the decision recognised in your community.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="hijri-guide-section" id="hijri-months" aria-labelledby="hijri-months-title">
  <h3 id="hijri-months-title">The twelve Hijri months and important observances</h3>
  <p><a href="https://quran.com/9/36" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 9:36</a> establishes twelve months, with four accorded sacred status. <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:4662" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 4662</a> names those four as Muharram, Rajab, Dhul Qa'dah and Dhul Hijjah. Calendar calculations are useful for advance planning, while observance dates may require local confirmation.</p>
  <ol class="hijri-month-list">{months.map((name,index)=><li><span>{name}</span>{[0,6,10,11].includes(index)&&<small>Sacred month</small>}{index===8&&<small>Ramadan fasting</small>}{index===9&&<small>Eid al-Fitr begins with Shawwal</small>}{index===11&&<small>Hajj and Eid al-Adha period</small>}</li>)}</ol>
  <p>Ramadan begins with the ninth lunar month and Eid al-Fitr with the tenth. Hajj rites and Eid al-Adha fall in Dhul Hijjah. The <a href="/tools/ramadan-timetable/">Ramadan timetable</a>, <a href="/tools/islamic-events-calendar/">Islamic events calendar</a> and <a href="/tools/white-days-calendar/">White Days planner</a> are planning aids, not independent moon-sighting announcements.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="hijri-guide-section" id="hijri-example" aria-labelledby="hijri-example-title">
  <h3 id="hijri-example-title">Worked conversion example</h3>
  <div class="hijri-worked-example"><p><strong>Example:</strong> Enter <strong>14 October 2017</strong> in Gregorian → Hijri and keep the Umm al-Qura method. The calculated result is <strong>24 Muharram 1439 AH</strong>, as also illustrated by <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Intl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MDN's Intl example</a>.</p>
   <p>Next, change to Hijri → Gregorian, select year 1439, month Muharram and day 24. Under Umm al-Qura the result returns to 14 October 2017. Switch to civil/tabular and compare: a difference demonstrates a changed *calendar algorithm*, not an official statement about which crescent was seen.</p>
   <p>Finally, inspect the selected month range. If the month has only 29 days, requesting day 30 should produce an invalid-date message instead of an invented 30th day.</p></div>
 </section>

 <section class="hijri-guide-section" id="hijri-sightings" aria-labelledby="hijri-sightings-title">
  <h3 id="hijri-sightings-title">Why local crescent announcements still matter</h3>
  <p><a href="https://quran.com/2/189" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 2:189</a> identifies lunar phases as a way to mark time. <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1909" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1909</a> connects Ramadan's start and end to the crescent and addresses overcast conditions. How communities evaluate and adopt sighting evidence is a matter for religious authorities, not a browser algorithm.</p>
  <p>For travel, school dates, work schedules or booking arrangements, consider an estimated Gregorian date and allow for possible local differences. Where a date is tied to fasting, Eid prayer, Hajj or another obligation, check a trusted mosque or national council rather than relying only on the converter. Some communities make announcements close to the evening itself.</p>
 </section>

